Today karr-foundation drains one board at a time. That is enough for independent repositories and not enough for a fleet whose repositories depend on each other, where several agent commands with different strengths are available, and where a human wants to be asked rather than surprised.

Full design: `karr get-refs refs/karr-foundation/spec/fleet-execution.md`

Three layers, karr owns the first two:
- Coordination (shared): tickets, dependencies, escalations, the chain, questions, the run log — all in git refs, synced.
- Execution (local): which agent commands exist here, whether they work, when to retry, how many at once. Never in the repository.
- Judgement (an agent): planning and routing, invoked only when a written plan is missing or has broken.

The AI is the compiler, the chain is the program, karr-foundation is the VM. No AI runs in the hot path.

Deliberately out of karr: everything domain-specific. What 'done' means for a project, how a release is verified, which project depends on which — that reaches karr through `on_drained` and nothing more. The fleet this came from is 44 Dist::Zilla distributions; karr must not learn a single thing about them.

Already present, no work needed: process-group kill (#148).
Work item 4 (#188) done: named agent definitions, the claude-code invocation contract, and per-agent availability. For the routing step the spec describes: the two inputs it needs are both plain local files today -- the config's 'agents:' section (command, kind, probe_every, prose description) and agents.state beside it (per agent: ok, or failing_since/next_attempt/last_error, plus a bounded list of recorded recoveries). There is deliberately no CLI surface wrapping them; if the coordination agent turns out to want one, that is a new ticket rather than a change to #188.
